-### Deep Image Matching Initialization
-An initialization pipeline that replaces traditional sparse SfM methods with a deep learning framework to construct a robust initial point cloud [S4]. It uses SuperPoint as the feature extractor to detect keypoints and compute descriptors, and LightGlue as the feature matcher to match descriptors between images [S4]. This approach replaces SfM/MVS which have limitations in robustness, efficiency, and scalability compared to deep learning methods [S4].
+Preprocessing and Initialization establishes the geometric foundation for volume rendering by estimating camera poses and generating an initial sparse or dense point cloud from input images. It solves the problem of providing accurate extrinsic parameters and a valid spatial prior for subsequent neural rendering or 3D reconstruction pipelines. What sets it apart from its siblings, [Canonical Frame Estimation via Facial Landmarks](16399-canonical-frame-estimation-via-facial-landmarks.md) and [Deep Image Matching Initialization](16400-deep-image-matching-initialization.md), is its reliance on traditional geometry-based multi-view stereo (MVS) techniques, such as feature matching and triangulation, often augmented with specific constraints like low-frequency masking or omnidirectional rectification, rather than purely learned canonical frames or deep matching networks.
 
 ### On-the-Fly SfM
-A method for online feature matching and pose estimation [S5]. It performs Hierarchical Weighted Local Bundle Adjustment (BA) incorporating only neighboring connected and registered images after acquiring a new image, reducing time complexity compared to global BA [S5]. It achieves online feature matching via global feature retrieval on HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World) to enable efficient dynamic updates for the matching matrix [S5].
+Traditional SfM frameworks (e.g., COLMAP) are incapable of processing images in an online manner. On-the-Fly SfM employs a Hierarchical Weighted Local Bundle Adjustment (BA), in which only neighboring connected and registered images are incorporated into BA after acquiring a new image, significantly reducing time complexity compared to global BA [S5]. It achieves online feature matching via global feature retrieval on HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World) to enable efficient dynamic updates for the matching matrix [S5].
+
+### Camera Parameter Learning
+A training strategy that enhances model robustness to camera parameters when small camera motion hinders standard SfM estimation (e.g., COLMAP) [S6]. It uses RCVD to predict initial camera parameters for the input video and simultaneously updates both camera parameters and MLPs during training to mitigate the impact of potentially inaccurate predictions [S6].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Incremental SfM is generally $O(N^2)$ for matching and $O(N \cdot M)$ for BA, where $N$ is the number of images and $M$ is the number of points. Global SfM (e.g., GLOMAP) can be dramatically quicker on large sets [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On a machine with an RTX A6000 GPU and 128 GB RAM:
-    *   For 310 frames: COLMAP took 00:08:26, HLOC took 01:08:39, LocalRF took 23:20:52 [S2].
-    *   For 1392 frames: COLMAP took 00:49:29, HLOC took 21:51:34, LocalRF took 75:10:54 [S2].
-    *   For 2784 frames: COLMAP took 04:32:53 [S2].
-*   **Scaling:** COLMAP performs well with dense views (2784 frames) but is slower than HLOC on sparse views (310, 1392 frames) [S2]. HLOC is slower but more effective on sparse views [S2]. LocalRF handles long-range trajectories but has extensive running time [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Robust to viewpoint changes; provides accurate initial poses for downstream optimization [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Struggles with repetitive textures, low-texture regions, and significant motion blur [S3, unofficial]. Requires sufficient overlap and baseline between images [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Incremental SfM scales poorly with the number of images due to $O(N^2)$ matching and expensive global BA. COLMAP is 8x faster than HLOC for 310 frames and 26.5x faster for 1392 frames [S3]. LocalRF is significantly slower than both [S3].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the full matching matrix and point cloud in memory.
+*   **Measured Performance:** On a machine with an RTX A6000 GPU and 128 GB RAM, COLMAP took 00:08:26 for 310 frames, 00:49:29 for 1392 frames, and 04:32:53 for 2784 frames [S3]. HLOC took 01:08:39 for 310 frames and 21:51:34 for 1392 frames [S3].
+*   **Strengths:** Produces explicit geometry (point clouds and meshes) [S1]. Robust to textureless regions if sufficient overlap exists. Standard tool (COLMAP) is widely supported [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Fails with sparse views (HLOC is better for sparse views) [S3]. Struggles with repetitive textures, reflections, and motion blur [S4, unofficial]. Not suitable for real-time online reconstruction without variants like On-the-Fly SfM [S5].
+*   **When to Use:** When high-quality explicit geometry is needed, or as a seed for Gaussian Splatting/NeRF training with dense views [S4, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** For real-time online applications (use On-the-Fly SfM), or when dealing with very sparse views where learned methods (HLOC) may perform better [S3].
 
 ## Background
-Structure-from-Motion is a classic computer vision technique that predates Gaussian Splats by decades [S3, unofficial]. It is the same math that builds the sparse point clouds in classic photogrammetry [S3, unofficial]. The de facto standard tool for SfM is COLMAP, an open-source package by Schönberger and Frahm (CVPR 2016) [S3, unofficial]. It is used to generate the camera poses and sparse point cloud that serve as the seed for Gaussian Splatting training [S3, unofficial].
+Structure-from-Motion is a technique to recover camera poses, camera calibration, and a sparse 3D point cloud from a set of uncalibrated photographs [S4, unofficial]. It is described as 'load-bearing' for Gaussian Splatting because the splats require known camera positions to optimize against the source images [S4, unofficial]. The de facto standard tool is COLMAP, an open-source SfM and Multi-View Stereo package by Schönberger and Frahm (CVPR 2016) [S4, unofficial]. It is used as a standard pipeline for heritage documentation and comparative baseline evaluation against NeRFs [S1].
 
